Q: How to make detailed Listviews in QML A: <p>I agree with @JarMan that you should set a ListView delegate. I also recommend using a different top-level component instead of Rectangle, such as ItemDelegate or Frame. If Rectangle is used as the top-level item, you’ll need to manually manage its width and height. Both ItemDelegate and Frame provide a background property for customizing their appearance. ItemDelegate also includes built-in text support and is clickable by default.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>ListView {
    model: [ &quot;Selection 1&quot;, &quot;Selection 2&quot;, &quot;Selection 3&quot;, &quot;Selection 4&quot;,
             &quot;Selection 5&quot;, &quot;Selection 6&quot;, &quot;Selection 7&quot; ]
    delegate: ItemDelegate {
        width: ListView.view.width
        background: RectangleWithGradient { }
        text: modelData
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I also recommend you refactor <code>RectangleWithGradient.qml</code> to reduce code complexity:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>// RectangleWithGradient.qml
import QtQuick
import QtQuick.Controls
Rectangle {
    property color fromColor: &quot;#d9d9d9&quot;
    property color toColor: &quot;#c2c2c2&quot;
    gradient: Gradient {
        GradientStop { position: 0; color: fromColor }
        GradientStop { position: 1; color: toColor }
        orientation: Gradient.Vertical
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can <a href="https://stephenquan.github.io/qmlonline/?" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Try it Online!</a></p>
